as vote counting got underway it  appeared ferdinand marcus jr was surging  ahead he's the son and namesake of the  former philippine president described by  many as a dictator and ousted in the  1986 revolution i know that the  account is not yet done  it's not just over but i wanted to issue  a short statement  and it essentially is a statement of  gratitude  to all of those who have been with us in  this long  and sometimes very difficult journey  ten candidates are running but for the  presidency it's widely considered a  two-person race  vice president leni robredo who is also  the leader of the opposition is trailing  behind marcus jr  initial results show a lead so wide poll  experts believe it is unlikely robredo  can catch up  i am with you in the belief that the  elections must respect the full voice  and will of the people  it is important that the people continue  their belief and respect for democracy  more than 60 million filipinos voted for  the successor to president rodrigo  duterte in one of asia's oldest  democracies  here in one of the biggest polling  precincts in manila voting day was  marked with frustration about  malfunctioning voting machines  people lined up for hours and refused to  leave even as the polls officially  closed  we want to be here we want to see our  ballots going through the machines and  see the receipts  we refuse to leave our posts this  election is too important and every vote  is sacred  the election commission assures the  public that it is very much in control  and the sanctity of the votes is still  protected  a marcus presidency would mean an  extraordinary return for a family that  was forcibly removed from power three  decades ago through people power and  could become the most consequential  election in recent philippine history  jamal lindogen al jazeera manila
 

